# Line endings, pinned rather than left to core.autocrlf.
#
# This is not hypothetical bookkeeping. On 2026-08-18 a sibling project on this same machine lost
# two full benchmark rounds because a shell script picked up CRLF on Windows: inside a Linux
# container `set -euo pipefail\r` becomes `pipefail: invalid option name` and the entrypoint dies
# in two seconds. YAML, Python, PowerShell and Dockerfiles tolerate CRLF; bash does not.
#
# So: text normalised in the repo, and anything a Linux shell will execute forced to LF on
# checkout regardless of platform.

* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Python is read by interpreters that accept either, but keeping it LF means a diff never shows a
# whole file changed because it was opened in a different editor.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Windows launchers must keep CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.safetensors binary
*.gguf binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.ckpt binary
*.png binary
*.pyd binary
